    <abstract>
      <?line 17?>

<t>A profile of RFC 6234's SHA-256 test results for sha256 in
nish/crypto/sha256 (std/crypto/sha256.ts). It holds every byte-length test
of Section 8.5 that vector can state, and properties of the digest's
length and of its dependence on every byte.</t>
    </abstract>
  </front>
  <middle>
    <?line 24?>

<section anchor="introduction">
      <name>Introduction</name>
      <t>The key words "<bcp14>MUST</bcp14>", "<bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>REQUIRED</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHALL</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHALL
NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHOULD</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHOULD NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>RECOMMENDED</bcp14>", "<bcp14>NOT RECOMMENDED</bcp14>",
"<bcp14>MAY</bcp14>", and "<bcp14>OPTIONAL</bcp14>" in this document are to be interpreted as
described in BCP 14 <xref target="RFC2119"/> <xref target="RFC8174"/> when, and only when, they
appear in all capitals, as shown here.</t>
      <?line -18?>

<t>nish/crypto/sha256 exports <tt>sha256</tt>, a one-shot digest of a byte array. RFC 6234 is profiled one module at a time: a vector contract has one module under test, and vector 0.6.0 refuses a row that calls a reference function ("a vector tests nish/crypto/sha512, the module under test, so call one of its functions"). This profile tests nish/crypto/sha256; SHA-1 and SHA-384/SHA-512 are profiled by rfc6234-sha1 and rfc6234-sha512.</t>
      <t>Section 8.5 prints its test inputs as C macros, some of them concatenations
of string literals, and its results as uppercase hexadecimal split across C
string literals, so vector cannot find a result's bytes in the RFC's text
("the 64-byte expect is not printed in rfc6234 §8.5"). Each vector therefore
takes its expected value from Python's hashlib, as a command
<tt>spec check --run-sources</tt> runs. The inputs are the bytes the driver's
macros spell, and each digest hashlib prints is the one Section 8.5 prints,
byte for byte; both were checked against the RFC's text before they were
written here.</t>
      <t>Not every Section 8.5 test is held:</t>
      <ul spacing="normal">
        <li>
          <t>Test 3 hashes "a" a million times. vector 0.6.0 refuses the literal <tt>1000000 × 61</tt>
 ("Call: a repeated run expands to at most 65536 bytes"),
and an Iterate row feeds each result back into the next call, so it
cannot state a million-byte input. The row is left out rather than marked
slow: it cannot be written at all.</t>
        </li>
        <li>
          <t>Tests 5, 7 and 9 hash messages whose length is not a whole number of
bytes (the extra-bits tests). <tt>sha256</tt>
takes a byte array, so there is no call that states them.</t>
        </li>
        <li>
          <t>SHA-224 has no export in nish's standard library.</t>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <t>Out of scope: Sha256, the streaming form of sha256; a vector row and a property here call only functions, so the class is left to nish's own tests (tests/run.js)</t>
      <t>Out of scope: SHA256_SIZE, a constant, not a function; the properties test the digest length it names</t>
      <t>Out of scope: SHA256_BLOCK, a constant, not a function; it names the block size the two-block vectors cross</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="interface">
      <name>Interface</name>
      <sourcecode type="ts-interface"><![CDATA[
// module: nish/crypto/sha256
export const sha256 = (data: u8[]): u8[]
]]></sourcecode>
    </section>
    <section anchor="the-function">
      <name>The Function</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-DIGEST">
        <t>When a message of any length &lt; 2^64 bits (for SHA-224 and SHA-256) or &lt; 2^128 bits (for SHA-384 and SHA-512) is input to one of these algorithms, the result is an output called a message digest.
<xref section="1" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC6234"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-SHA256-OUT">
        <t>For SHA-256, this is the concatenation of all of H(N)0, H(N)1, through H(N)7.
<xref section="6.2" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC6234"/></t>
      </blockquote>
    </section>
    <section anchor="the-test-driver">
      <name>The Test Driver</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-DRIVER">
        <t>The following code is a main program test driver to exercise the code in sha1.c, sha224-256.c, sha384-512.c, hmac.c, and hkdf.c.
<xref section="8.5" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC6234"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-ASCII">
        <t>Note that the tests assume that character values are as in [US-ASCII] and a run time check warns if the code appears to have been compiled with some other character system.
<xref section="8.5" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC6234"/></t>
      </blockquote>
    </section>
